{"cluster_id": "C-0513", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B040-194"], "canonical_claim": "Fsck can report clean after an incomplete hot frontier because it silently skips absent snapshot logs and disappearing covered logs and cannot detect a whole omitted namespace", "issue_ids": ["Q7", "Q9"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r1_findings.md#L23-L25"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0514", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B040-197"], "canonical_claim": "RefTxnId allocation can wrap ref_sequence at UINT64_MAX, making a real log id indistinguishable from a seal anchor; the protocol also needs prev < txn_id, cycle detection, and a resource bound", "issue_ids": ["Q1", "Q10"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r1_findings.md#L32-L34"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0515", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B040-219"], "canonical_claim": "A NeverBorn recovery seal needs a specified zero sealed_from exception and seal-aware fsck handling; otherwise a healthy pool whose newest snapshot is synthetic remains unchecked", "issue_ids": ["N2", "N8"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r3_findings.md#L28-L30"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0516", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B040-223"], "canonical_claim": "An absent _seal_latest cannot prove clean history because deletion, rollback, or loss can make a published authority appear absent; absence must be a hold or corruption condition", "issue_ids": ["Q1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r4_findings.md#L7-L9"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0517", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B011-100", "B041-002", "B041-024", "B041-061"], "canonical_claim": "After any ambiguous send, a later definite rejection does not prove a ref ID is reusable because the earlier request can still land and occupy the key. Additionally, When an ambiguous ref append's exact GET remains absent, the current wedge can remain Unresolved forever and keep the namespace unavailable until an externally induced remount.; An uncertain ref-log create is never treated as failed when an exact-key GET finds absence; the append lane remains wedged until identical bytes are observed, a whitelisted definite rejection is proven, or the writer unmounts.; A permanent ref-append wedge is demand-driven: without a later caller it performs no retry, and an independently occurring remount is only one possible escape; the unresolved operation was not acknowledged.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r5_findings.md#L13-L23", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r6_findings.md#L74-L82", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r8_findings.md#L111-L113", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-11-cas-ref-table-snapshot-log-design.md#writer-side-linearization"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0518", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B041-041", "B050-052", "B050-053"], "canonical_claim": "Incarnation-qualified physical keys alone do not make a hot-reader catalog mismatch rejection guarantee true because a reader without a catalog read, local fence transition, or bounded refresh cannot observe a remote replacement. Additionally, Resolving a namespace from the current catalog on every stale read lets a stale reader observe successor bytes; retaining the open-time `NamespaceLifeId` prevents that defect. Additionally, Capturing only a namespace name in a table-file `CaInlineWriteBuffer` and resolving it during finalization can change life 2's token and replace `life-2-stable\\n` with `life-1-delayed\\n`; retaining the open-time `NamespaceLifeId` prevents this defect.", "issue_ids": ["T6"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task6-ns-file-contract-report.md#L17-L25", ".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task6-ns-file-contract-report.md#L20-L27", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r7_findings.md#L67-L71"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0519", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B041-109"], "canonical_claim": "Path reconstruction is fragile because the abstraction boundary erases the MergeTree object structure, requiring independently evolving path grammars to continue agreeing.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/datapartstorage-content-addressed-design-concern.md#L18-L34"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0520", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B043-008"], "canonical_claim": "Issue `B140` was caused by `reuseBlob` transferring a blob hash without transferring its `cas_owner` protection, allowing GC to delete a blob still referenced by a committed part.", "issue_ids": ["B140"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-b140"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0521", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B043-024"], "canonical_claim": "An asynchronous condemn-marker write failure must not be swallowed: graduation to `delete_pending` requires confirmed durable `Condemned` evidence, otherwise the entry carries to a later round.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-three-oracles"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0522", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B043-027"], "canonical_claim": "An acknowledged-then-lost `INSERT` was fixed by closing the disk transaction in `renameParts`, making the part durable before the Keeper commit that acknowledges it.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-dataloss-txn"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0523", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B043-066"], "canonical_claim": "Concurrent FETCH fan-out can tear the shared `detached` ref on `LocalObjectStorage`, because readers race an in-place truncating rewrite; S3 PUTs are atomic and do not have this specific tear.", "issue_ids": ["B66a"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/random_notes.md#lines-348-348"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0524", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B043-069"], "canonical_claim": "A concurrent ref drop during mutable-only commit can throw through `MergeTreeTransaction::afterCommit`, whose `noexcept` boundary calls `std::terminate`; the race needs a recoverable no-op or an earlier detection path.", "issue_ids": ["B89"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/random_notes.md#lines-356-356"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0525", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B017-038", "B043-071"], "canonical_claim": "`RunRef::checksum` is serialized but not validated by consumers, while block and footer CRC32C remains the only checked streaming corruption guard; removing CRC32C before enforcing the run checksum would weaken detection.; Every `cas_run` consumer must stream the whole object, accumulate its checksum, and verify it against the referencing `RunRef.checksum` before the records affect a deletion decision.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-15-cas-codecs-v3-phase5-runs.md#global-constraints", "docs/superpowers/random_notes.md#lines-387-398"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0526", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B044-006"], "canonical_claim": "The `allow_stale` resolve path can use a ref dropped up to the 200 ms cache TTL ago, but no assertion enforces that `shard_decode_cache_ttl_ms` is shorter than GC condemn-to-graduate latency.", "issue_ids": ["R3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L137-L141"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0527", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B044-010"], "canonical_claim": "The flat-combining write queue can block every writer queued on one `(namespace, shard)` behind a stalled leader, and a transient leader failure can fail the entire carved batch.", "issue_ids": ["W-N3"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L220-L231"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0528", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B045-008"], "canonical_claim": "An incorrect `SYSTEM CAS GC REBUILD` can cause a later GC round to delete still-referenced objects because the rebuilt baseline drives subsequent retire decisions.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/en/sql-reference/statements/system.md#L480-L483"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0529", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B046-032"], "canonical_claim": "Before the fix round, the post-listing post_listing_cut.life_index.resolve(candidate.life_id) call was uncaught, so an ambiguous catalog life ID threw CORRUPTED_DATA and aborted fsck instead of recording a lifeless_keys finding; the corrected code catches CORRUPTED_DATA, records the key, and continues.", "issue_ids": ["F1"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/t3-review.md#L316-L338", ".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/t3-review.md#L43-L82"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0530", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B049-010"], "canonical_claim": "Deriving baseline cursor authority from the full successor set incorrectly treats a catalog-only row as a parent cursor and bypasses `CasRefGc.BaselineGuardRefusesWhenSnapshotSurvivesWithoutLogsOrCursor`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-07-28-cas-ref-chain-stage-b-catalog/task-5-checkpoint75b-round-plan-report.md#implemented-boundary"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0531", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B050-062"], "canonical_claim": "Routing `CatalogAfterListBackend`'s simulated concurrent catalog probe through `CountingBackend::get` caused `CasNamespaceJanitor.PostListCatalogCutProtectsConcurrentCreationWithOneGet` to observe two gets instead of one; the probe must use `InMemoryBackend::get` and document the intentional bypass.", "issue_ids": ["T8"], "sources": [".superpowers/sdd/2026-08-02-cas-stage-b-remaining/tidy-fix-report.md#L29-L47"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0532", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B051-004"], "canonical_claim": "Using early projection or sub-part commits to make staged files readable creates windows in which orphan temporary metadata or blobs can remain after an exception.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L12-L13"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0533", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B051-028", "B051-065"], "canonical_claim": "`LocalObjectStorage::listObjects` could throw a filesystem exception if a file disappeared between directory iteration and the subsequent `file_size` or metadata stat, whereas an object store would omit the vanished object. Additionally, `LocalObjectStorage::tryGetObjectMetadata` must treat directories as non-objects and a file or directory that vanishes during metadata lookup as `nullopt`, matching object-store snapshot semantics.", "issue_ids": ["B38"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream-patch-inventory.md#local-object-storage-cpp", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L208-L218"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0534", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B051-041"], "canonical_claim": "Single-table `SYSTEM` commands that cast directly to a table must unwrap `TableProxy` first; otherwise a `DETACH`/`ATTACH` followed by `SYNC REPLICA` can fail even without CAS.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L380-L380"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0535", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-132", "B051-043"], "canonical_claim": "A buffer-constructor deadlock affected the rename forwarder; the rename forwarder was reverted while the mutation forwarder remained and was covered by test 05021. Additionally, A codex-only finding in the xhigh review identified that the rename-forward change in `StorageTableProxy` caused a Buffer-constructor DEADLOCK, requiring the rename forward to be reverted (xfix item D).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L382-L383", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-21-unattended-reftablestate-experiments.md#timeline"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0536", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["B051-046", "B051-048"], "canonical_claim": "The in-memory part image without a carried-forward projection can cause `MergedBlockOutputStream.cpp` to finalize checksums from the wrong image, `MergeTask.cpp` to make the wrong projection decision, and `DataPartsExchange.cpp` to omit the projection during fetch. Additionally, The `registerCarriedForwardProjectionForCA` workaround bypassed the disk transaction and led to B63, where a carried-forward projection had `rows_count=0` and a `SELECT` silently lost part of an aggregate.", "issue_ids": ["B63"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L338-L342", "docs/superpowers/cas/upstream.md#L346-L349"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0537", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-081"], "canonical_claim": "An R5-round review found a CRITICAL trust-flip bug where finding #19's guard made `CasProbe` vacuous on Native S3 pools: wrong-dialect probe tokens were being rejected locally before ever going over the wire, at three call sites in `CasProbe.cpp:93,143,172`; the fix applies a live-dialect probe at all three sites plus a reachability test that pins non-vacuousness.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-17-unattended-r5-r6-round.md#L241-L247"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0538", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-133"], "canonical_claim": "A StorageProxy virtual-forwarding audit found roughly 60 unforwarded virtuals (about 45 that must be forwarded), including a CRITICAL silent-empty `backupData`; `mutate` forwarding was correct, rename forwarding moved to `StorageTableProxy`, and `detach` forwarding was dropped.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-21-unattended-reftablestate-experiments.md#timeline"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0539", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-134"], "canonical_claim": "Three distinct lazy-proxy bugs were found during this round: (1) `StorageProxy` forwarded `mutate` but not `checkMutationIsPossible`, so a lazy proxy rejected all mutations (`MATERIALIZE TTL` -> `NOT_IMPLEMENTED`); (2) the proxy's cached metadata lacked TTL info, breaking `MATERIALIZE TTL` a second way after the first fix; both were fixed and validated by the narrowed stateless test 05021.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-21-unattended-reftablestate-experiments.md#timeline"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0540", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-176"], "canonical_claim": "The current fsck oracle assumes a snapshot ID is also a log ID and silently skips a recovery seal on that assumption, rather than reporting the skip as checked; and repeated per-held-namespace anchor/repair GETs on every folding round are rate-bounded only by the scheduler, with no cumulative bound or backoff.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-28-ref-rework-reviews/codex_r2_findings.md#L125-L136"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0541", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-195"], "canonical_claim": "SEC-4: a pool-write-capable party (or a corrupt/huge object) can place a valid-CRC, valid-framing but enormous shard or manifest directly in S3; decoders (`decodeRootShard`, `decodePartManifest`) validate magic/version/CRC/journal-integrity but a valid giant body still allocates on decode, causing OOM in readers and the GC folder (GC folds whole journals). Recommendation: enforce absolute decode-side size/element caps independent of the write path, and stream large journals in the fold.", "issue_ids": ["SEC-4"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L992-L1001"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-0542", "topic": "CAS correctness", "target": "architecture/correctness", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-196"], "canonical_claim": "SEC-5: key builders route through `checkNamespace`, `namespaceFileKey`, and `mountpointObjectKey`, which reject empty segments, the reserved `_files`/`_manifests`/`_precommits` names, `..`, leading/trailing `/`, and `//`, but `checkNamespace` itself does not reject a `..` segment; this is safe only because the backend is an object store with literal (non-normalized) keys, so a filesystem-backed or path-normalizing backend would be vulnerable to traversal. Recommendation: reject `..` in `checkNamespace` so the invariant does not silently depend on backend key semantics.", "issue_ids": ["SEC-5"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews.md#L1003-L1012"]}

